You can add a block in your Nginx configuration to rewrite the URL for each post. Here's an example of how you can do it:

Add a new `location` block inside your `server` block:
```
location ~ ^/post/([0-9]+)/?$ {
    rewrite ^/post/([0-9]+)/$ /post/$1 permanent;
}
```
This block will match URLs that start with `/post/` followed by any number of digits and a trailing slash. The `rewrite` directive will replace the URL with the original value, added to the end of the `/post/` prefix.

For example, a URL like `/post/123` will be rewritten to `/post/123`, and a URL like `/post/123/` will be rewritten to `/post/123`.

You can also add a `return` directive to specify the URL that should be returned for the rewritten URL. For example:
```
location ~ ^/post/([0-9]+)/?$ {
    rewrite ^/post/([0-9]+)/$ /post/$1 permanent;
    return 301 https://www.askfundu.com/post/$1;
}
```
This will redirect any URL that starts with `/post/` to the corresponding URL with the `https` protocol and the `www` subdomain.

Make sure to update your `try_files` directive to include the rewritten URL:
```
location / {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html index.htm;
    try_files $uri $uri/ /post/$uri /index.html;
}
```
This will try to serve files from the `/post` directory for URLs that match the rewritten pattern.

You can also add a block to serve the post page directly:
```
